💣 Payloads Used
The main payload in this lab is the MFA (2FA) code brute-force list.
Since most labs use a 4-digit numeric code, your payload looks like:

0000
0001
0002
0003
...
9999

This is configured in Burp Intruder:
Attack type: Sniper (or simple brute-force)
Payload position: mfa-code
Parameter manipulated: verify=carlos
👉 The goal is to try all possible codes until one works.

🧪 What the Payload Actually Does

The payload systematically sends thousands of requests, each with a different 2FA code. Because:

There is no rate limiting
No account lockout
No proper session binding
The server accepts repeated attempts without blocking.
Eventually:
One request has the correct code
Server responds with 302 redirect
That means authentication success ✅

🚨 Why This Is Dangerous

This vulnerability completely breaks 2FA security. Even though 2FA is supposed to add an extra layer of protection, this flaw allows attackers to
Bypass authentication
Access other users’ accounts
Perform full account takeover

👉 In real-world apps, this would be critical severity (P1 bug)

🔍 Key Security Mistakes
The application fails because:
It trusts user input (verify parameter)
It doesn’t bind 2FA to session/user securely
It allows unlimited brute-force attempts
It lacks monitoring or rate limiting

🛡️ How to Prevent This
To fix this issue, developers should:
Bind 2FA verification strictly to the logged-in session
Ignore user-controlled identifiers like verify
Implement rate limiting for 2FA attempts
Add account lockouts after multiple failures
Use secure tokens instead of predictable parameters

🧠 Final Insight

 teaches a powerful lesson:
👉 Security is not just about adding features (like 2FA), but implementing them correctly there would be no place for a flaw.

Even strong protections become useless if the logic behind them is flawed.
